ter manchester but only pay  people in those businesses 67  of their wages and let's remember they  were people working in pubs in  bookmakers in bingo halls i said that  wasn't right  when office workers had been given  four-fifths of their wages earlier in  the year  and also they were offering no support  whatsoever  to people who are self-employed and on  both of those fronts i said you cannot  take away  people's livelihoods and not help them  manage in this situation it was a point  of principle okay  i took a stand i'm glad i did i would do  it again  of course the government has since  conceded on those points  and there is now a proper furlough  scheme back in place and the support for  people who are self-employed  it just seems to me that you know they  can't leave it alone now they want to  they want to come back at it and they  want to to punish me and blame me for  for everything can't we just agree that  there was a difference of opinion it was  resolved  and now we all must look forward to  getting things right going forward  that's that's the right way for everyone  to to go about things  but it's almost a bit of divide and  conquer isn't it because  not only um did that we have that veiled  attack on you we also heard how well  liverpool had done  um during uh movement into tier three  um in addition to that we're being told  that regional mayors will have no  influence whatsoever on what's going to  happen on thursday when we find out  which tier system  we're in i wonder if you have a view on  both of those points  well you're right on the um divide and  rule points it's clearly what they're  doing isn't it and everyone can see what  they're doing  certainly here in the northwest uh in  liverpool they  they can read between those lines uh no  problem at all and everyone can see the  the government's tactics there but more  broadly you know why are they punishing  everybody because they had a route with  us  but but as i said it was a row that  needed to be had because we  helped win a better support package for  very low paid workers and people who are  self-employed and i'm glad we did take  that  that stand but it doesn't say much about  the government that the minute their  challenge k they just then have to say  oh well  i'm sorry but i can't work with anybody  we're just going to sort of bulldoze our  way  our way through you know that that isn't  the way to go about things and actually  if you talk to steve brotherham  and you talk to people in lancashire and  talk to people in sheffield  they felt first time round uh things  were railroaded through and there wasn't  a real negotiation or a real discussion  so i'm not sure they've changed  at all to be honest but there is a  danger here  they're gonna get a reputation if  they're not careful of being quite a  bullying government if they don't get  their own way  they take their their toys home their  ball home and then they just try and  impose their will  that i don't think is is a responsible  way to govern there are going to be  disagreements they created  these positions that that i hold you  know there's no point in having  devolution  if you just sort of you know you you  kind of bow down and you just do as  you're told all the time governments get  things wrong  and sometimes governments need to be  challenged and i won't be able to look  my residence here in great manchester in  the eye  if i sold them down the river just  because the government told me that's  what they were  going to do so whitehall and westminster  needs to  needs to sort of decide here they need  to grow up do they want evolution or do  they not  because sometimes devolution is needed  to be a check and balance in the system  to stop governments bulldozing their  will on the people because as we all  know it's the north of england  that have been at the sharp end of  government's behaviors over many many  years  we are finding our voice now we are not  going to be  pushed 